Speaker 1: Alright, and so, it's, you know, this is very similar to the smart TV situation, where there's a built-in OS that sucks, you have no control over, you know, if it broke, no one could really fix it.

535
00:46:07,150 --> 00:46:29,337
Speaker 1: But, even on a smart TV, now having a smart TV, you can just disable and ignore pretty much all of that, and use something, you know, I could use the Apple TV, I could use, I could just watch channels with the antenna, I don't actually need to use any of the smart TV functions ever, I could ignore it, block it, and disable it to a great extent, never see the ads it's trying to show, because they're

536
00:46:29,357 --> 00:46:30,418
Speaker 0: blocked,

537
00:46:30,438 --> 00:46:39,770
Speaker 1: and you're still getting full use of the TV, there is no feature of the TV that is essential, that you're not accessing, because you're not using the OS that came with it.

538
00:46:39,810 --> 00:46:48,090
Speaker 1: But if you have a car, it's like, okay, you can drive the car, and in RIM's case, have HVAC, you know, temperature control, climate control.

539
00:46:48,411 --> 00:46:50,489
Speaker 0: I was lucky, if it was a Tesla, that wouldn't have worked.

540
00:46:51,250 --> 00:46:51,451
Speaker 1: Right.

541
00:46:51,851 --> 00:47:10,323
Speaker 1: But, you know, navigation, and, you know, I guess you can hear, if you're using your phone instead, okay, but the sound system is not working, because the computer sucks, it's like, that is sort of a core function of the car, that is now not working, and you can't do anything about it.

542
00:47:20,550 --> 00:47:24,349
Speaker 1: They're forcing you to bring it in to get it repaired, it's like, no, they can't even repair it.

543
00:47:24,750 --> 00:47:34,530
Speaker 1: It's like, could you imagine, like, my iPhone broke, shit, there's no right to repair, I can't go to a third party, I have to take it to Apple, you bring it to Apple, they're like, yeah, we can't fix that either.

544
00:47:34,950 --> 00:47:37,200
Speaker 1: It's like, buy a whole new car, just because the radio's not working.

545
00:47:39,771 --> 00:47:41,789
Speaker 0: And three weeks, and we might be able to fix it.

546
00:47:42,351 --> 00:47:55,610
Speaker 1: Right, it's like, buy a whole new car, because the radio's not working, or swap out the whole computer in the car, because the mechanic can't possibly, like, you know, flash the OS, or actually investigate it in any way as a computer expert.

547
00:47:56,591 --> 00:48:05,290
Speaker 0: And the funniest part is, some of the problems are caused by the fact that all these systems have basic interfaces, because everyone has either an Android phone or an iOS phone.

548
00:48:05,911 --> 00:48:13,270
Speaker 0: So as a result, Android and iOS each have a "I am talking to a car" feature, that is designed to standardize this.

549
00:48:13,270 --> 00:48:16,090
Speaker 1: The carplay, or whatever Google calls theirs, they're the same thing.

550
00:48:16,270 --> 00:48:25,750
Speaker 0: The problem is, even if that software works, Apple and Android both update that software, but the car people don't update theirs, nor do they even do a lot of QA.

551
00:48:26,570 --> 00:48:31,750
Speaker 1: Right, I mean, this is one of the reasons I believe Apple was trying to make a car, they failed.

552
00:48:32,990 --> 00:48:41,470
Speaker 1: But the one advantage would be that, like, yeah, if they had made a car, then this aspect would have been more acceptable.

553
00:48:42,111 --> 00:48:50,368
Speaker 1: At least if you had an Apple device of some kind, which I assume you would, because who the fuck buys an Apple car and has Windows computers and Android phones?

554
00:48:50,849 --> 00:48:51,110
Speaker 1: Nobody.

555
00:48:53,272 --> 00:49:07,910
Speaker 1: Apple did abandon their plans for the car, it seems, which probably I'm going to assume for good reasons, but I think there was some partnership with Apple and certain car brands that has come and gone at times.

556
00:49:08,010 --> 00:49:24,510
Speaker 1: I think one car brand, I don't forget, recently got rid of it, but I think it would behoove them to either just stop trying to make a fancy car that didn't work, just make a normal car, and do the computer part right, and that will have appeal on its own.

557
00:49:24,590 --> 00:49:32,530
Speaker 1: You don't need to go wild with some nonsense, and find a car brand that will, you know, cooperate with you.

558
00:49:32,570 --> 00:49:35,983
Speaker 0: Harder with you and make a car that just works.

559
00:49:36,063 --> 00:49:37,709
Speaker 0: the way Apple phones just work.

560
00:49:38,690 --> 00:49:40,889
Speaker 1: I walk in, I set my Apple phone down, and then it just works.

561
00:49:41,070 --> 00:49:45,629
Speaker 1: I'm not trying to Apple fanboy here, because I got an iPhone and all Mac devices right now.

562
00:49:46,311 --> 00:49:47,352
Speaker 1: Google, do the same thing.

563
00:49:47,612 --> 00:50:00,030
Speaker 1: Google, go find, like Google and Samsung, right, it's like, go ahead, find a car manufacturer that, you know, you can make a deal with, and be like, listen, we're going to handle the OS for your car.

564
00:50:00,631 --> 00:50:02,175
Speaker 1: You can stop doing it, right?

565
00:50:02,235 --> 00:50:04,460
Speaker 1: We're going to do it, and that will help you.

566
00:50:04,500 --> 00:50:05,342
Speaker 1: just let us do that.

567
00:50:05,382 --> 00:50:07,427
Speaker 1: That'll save you money on development.

568
00:50:07,488 --> 00:50:08,350
Speaker 1: You won't have to do that.

569
00:50:09,091 --> 00:50:18,324
Speaker 1: And two, you will, and then make money, because people will out there will be like, oh, that car has a computer system that doesn't fucking suck.

570
00:50:18,424 --> 00:50:22,170
Speaker 1: Google made it, and Google keeps updating it, and we can trust it a little bit more.

571
00:50:22,771 --> 00:50:25,138
Speaker 1: And Google, how are they going to make money on this?

572
00:50:25,379 --> 00:50:27,225
Speaker 1: Of course, collecting all your data from your car.

573
00:50:27,706 --> 00:50:28,750
Speaker 0: Come on, come on.

574
00:50:28,990 --> 00:50:34,390
Speaker 0: And then Apple will make money by also doing that, but with a veneer of privacy, and charging the company money.

575
00:50:34,550 --> 00:50:41,670
Speaker 1: Apple will make money on it, because it will have very limited features unless you pay the subscription fee to CarCloud.

576
00:50:42,170 --> 00:50:42,290
Speaker 0: Yep.

577
00:50:43,792 --> 00:51:01,210
Speaker 0: But I think these are, I would say that sound system is a necessity, and it's dangerous to not have one for people who drive a lot, because one of the main reasons to have a sound system is to put on music or some sort of content to keep yourself awake if you're the kind of person who has to drive a long road trip by yourself.

578
00:51:02,073 --> 00:51:06,430
Speaker 1: Yeah, I guess so, but I think even more important is simply just traffic and weather information.

579
00:51:07,211 --> 00:51:07,809
Speaker 0: Yep, navigating.

580
00:51:08,930 --> 00:51:09,210
Speaker 1: Yes.

581
00:51:09,731 --> 00:51:31,110
Speaker 1: So, I think that I agree, there should be, just like a cell phone that has no service can still call 911, I believe there should be some regulation that requires cars, no matter how busted the computer part is, there should be some AM radio functionality, there should be the base set of functionality that should always work.

582
00:51:31,391 --> 00:51:37,230
Speaker 1: Even if the computer dash thing is like fried, like on fire magic smoke came out.

583
00:51:38,390 --> 00:51:40,330
Speaker 1: And the rest, assuming the rest of the car is still just fine.

584
00:51:40,370 --> 00:51:47,170
Speaker 0: Because I mean, I drove a car six miles without a serpentine belt not that long ago, like cars are pretty durable.

585
